<p dir=3D"auto">In react when we use class based components we get access t=
o lifecycle methods ( like <code>componentDidMount()</code>, `componentDidU=
pdate(), etc ). But when we want use a functional component and also we wan=
t to use lifecycle methods, then using useEffect() we can implement those l=
ifecycle methods.</p>
<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>1. componentDidMount():</strong></p>
<p dir=3D"auto">The <code>componentDidMount()</code> and <code>useEffect()<=
/code> run after the mount. However useEffect() runs after the paint has be=
en committed to the screen as opposed to before. This means we would get a =
flicker if needed to read from the DOM, then synchronously set state to mak=
e new UI.</p>
<p dir=3D"auto">The <code>useLayoutEffect()</code> was designed to have the=
 same timing as componentDidMount(). So <code>useLayoutEffect(fn, [])</code=
> is a much closer match to componentDidMount() than useEffect(fn, []) -- a=
t least from a timing standpoint.</p>
