<p dir=3D"auto">In React, <code>setState()</code> is asynchronous instead o=
f synchronous to optimize performance and ensure a smooth user experience. =
When you call <code>setState()</code>, React does not immediately update th=
e state and re-render the component. Instead, it batches multiple state upd=
ates together and schedules a re-render for later, often after the current =
event loop finishes.</p>
<p dir=3D"auto">This batching helps avoid unnecessary renders and keeps the=
 UI responsive, especially when many state updates happen in quick successi=
on (like during user input or network events). If <code>setState()</code> w=
ere synchronous, each update would trigger an immediate re-render, which co=
uld make the app slow and unresponsive.</p>
