<p dir=3D"auto">The key rule is: <strong>never call <code>.sort()</code> di=
rectly on the state array</strong>, because <code>Array.prototype.sort()</c=
ode> sorts in place and mutates the original array. Instead, create a shall=
ow copy first using the spread operator (<code>[...arr]</code>) or <code>.s=
lice()</code>, then sort the copy.</p>
<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>Why mutation is dangerous in React:</strong></p>
<p dir=3D"auto">React uses reference equality to detect state changes. If y=
ou mutate the existing array, the reference stays the same and React may no=
t re-render. You also lose the ability to roll back to a previous state.</p=
>
