<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>Prop drilling</strong> is when data is passed throu=
gh multiple intermediate component layers via props just to reach a deeply =
nested child that needs it =E2=80=94 even though the components in between =
don't use that data themselves.</p>
<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>Why it's a problem:</strong></p>
<ul dir=3D"auto">
<li><strong>Maintenance:</strong> Changing the data structure requires upda=
ting every component in the chain.</li>
<li><strong>Complexity:</strong> It makes components harder to reuse becaus=
e they are tightly coupled to the props they pass down.</li>
<li><strong>Readability:</strong> It becomes difficult to track where data =
is coming from in large trees.</li>
</ul>
<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>Using Context API:</strong></p>
<p dir=3D"auto">The Context API solves some of these prop drilling problems=
. It let pass data to all of the components in the tree without writing the=
m manually in each of them. Shared data can be anything: state, functions, =
objects, we name it, and it is accessible to all nested levels that are in =
the scope of the context.</p>
<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>Example:</strong></p>
