<p dir=3D"auto">A service worker is a background worker that acts as a prog=
rammable proxy, allowing us to control what happens on a request-by-request=
 basis. We can use it to make (parts of, or even entire) React apps work of=
fline.</p>
<p dir=3D"auto">Service workers depend on two APIs to work effectively: <co=
de>Fetch</code> (a standard way to retrieve content from the network) and <=
code>Cache</code> (content storage for application data. This cache is inde=
pendent from the browser cache or network status).</p>
<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>Service Worker Lifecycle:</strong></p>
<p dir=3D"auto">Each service worker goes through three steps in its lifecyc=
le: registration, installation and activation.</p>
<p align=3D"center" dir=3D"auto">
  <a target=3D"_blank" rel=3D"noopener noreferrer" href=3D"https://github.c=
om/learning-zone/react-basics/blob/master/assets/service-worker.png"><img s=
rc=3D"https://github.com/learning-zone/react-basics/raw/master/assets/servi=
ce-worker.png" alt=3D"Service Worker Lifecycle" width=3D"400px" style=3D"ma=
x-width: 100%;"></a>
</p>
<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>Registration:</strong></p>
<p dir=3D"auto">To install a service worker, you need to register it in scr=
ipt. Registration informs the browser where your service worker is located =
and lets it know it can start installing in the background.</p>
<p dir=3D"auto"><strong>Example:</strong> Basic registration in your <code>=
index.html</code> could look like this</p>
